<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#northern-black-polished-ware">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern Black Polished Ware</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700–200 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Northern Black Polished Ware is a fine, glossy black or grey pottery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, made from about 700 to 200 BCE. Found mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, it marks the period of the second urbanisation, when cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kausambi">Kaushambi</a> grew and punch-marked coins came into use. Its high-quality finish suggests that it was a luxury ware.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northern-black-polished-ware.jpg" alt="Northern Black Polished Ware" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Polished_Black_Ware_Culture_(700-200_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
